{"from": "Nariway Signals <signals@nariway.com>", "to": ["alina@nariway.com"], "subject": "Nariway Signals - 2026-08-25 - the \"boomer art dump\" no one's ready for", "html": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<title>Nariway Signals \u2014 August 25, 2026</title>\n<link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com\">\n<link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://fonts.gstatic.com\" crossorigin>\n<link href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Newsreader:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;1,400&family=Manrope:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Schibsted+Grotesk:wght@400;500;600&display=swap\" rel=\"stylesheet\">\n</head>\n<body style=\"margin:0;padding:0;background-color:#FFFFFF;color:#0A0A0A;font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;\">\n<table role=\"presentation\" width=\"100%\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" border=\"0\" style=\"width:100%;background-color:#FFFFFF;margin:0;padding:0;\">\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:0;\">\n<table role=\"presentation\" width=\"100%\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" border=\"0\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:600px;margin:0 auto;\">\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width:100%;max-width:600px;margin:0 auto;padding:36px 20px 24px 20px;\">\n\n<!-- MASTHEAD -->\n<table role=\"presentation\" width=\"100%\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" border=\"0\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;margin-bottom:6px;\">\n<tr>\n<td style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;color:#1A1A17;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.01em;\">\nNariway Signals\n</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n<div style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#6B6B62;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;margin-bottom:22px;\">\nTuesday, August 25, 2026\n</div>\n\n<div style=\"height:1px;line-height:1px;font-size:1px;background-color:#E7E5DF;margin:0 0 22px 0;\">&nbsp;</div>\n\n<!-- ORIENTATION -->\n<p style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-style:italic;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 30px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nHeaviest today on the gap between the &ldquo;great wealth transfer&rdquo; story the trade tells itself and what the market can actually absorb &mdash; a Bloomberg newsletter names that gap plainly, right as a Tampa developer&rsquo;s $28M gift and a wave of museum&#8209;leadership churn show individual institutions still growing to meet the very moment the skeptics say the field isn&rsquo;t ready for.\n</p>\n\n<!-- LEAD EYEBROW -->\n<div style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#6B6B62;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;margin-bottom:10px;\">\nLead\n</div>\n\n<h1 style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:600;font-size:28px;line-height:1.25;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 14px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nBloomberg: &ldquo;No one is ready for the great boomer art dump&rdquo;\n</h1>\n\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#0A0A0A;margin:0 0 12px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nThe newsletter&rsquo;s Aug 1 piece, following its July 31 feature putting the coming art-and-collectibles transfer at roughly $1 trillion, argues plainly that there aren&rsquo;t enough buyers or museums to absorb what boomer collectors are about to pass on &mdash; the mainstream-press version of the exact tension the claims register tracks (ownership rotation, not a sale wave), and the direct counterpart to Tim Schneider&rsquo;s &ldquo;reddest herring&rdquo; pushback logged here earlier this week. Read the two together: Schneider says the trade has mythologized the transfer into a rescue story; Bloomberg says even taken at face value, the absorption capacity isn&rsquo;t there. Neither is obviously wrong, and the daylight between them is the more interesting story than either piece alone.\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 30px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-08-01/no-one-is-ready-for-the-great-boomer-art-dump\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">Bloomberg</a>\n&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;\n<a href=\"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/features/2026-07-31/the-great-wealth-transfer-includes-1-trillion-in-art\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">Bloomberg, &ldquo;$1 Trillion in Art&rdquo;</a>\n</p>\n\n<div style=\"height:1px;line-height:1px;font-size:1px;background-color:#E7E5DF;margin:0 0 28px 0;\">&nbsp;</div>\n\n<!-- WHAT'S HAPPENING -->\n<div style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#6B6B62;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;margin-bottom:16px;\">\nWhat&rsquo;s happening\n</div>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:600;font-size:20px;line-height:1.3;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 8px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nA Tampa developer just made Florida&rsquo;s largest-ever private museum gift\n</h2>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#0A0A0A;margin:0 0 10px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nDick and Cornelia Corbett&rsquo;s $28M lead gift anchors the Tampa Museum of Art&rsquo;s Centennial Campaign (over $100M raised toward more than doubling the building, 69,000 to 120,000 sq ft) and directly unlocked TMA&rsquo;s first full-scale ancient Egypt show. A pattern this brief keeps seeing at every scale, from Lucas to Malba to here: a living patron using a capital campaign, not a personal-collection gift, as the legacy vehicle.\n</p>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 26px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<a href=\"https://tampamuseum.org/tma-opens-first-ancient-egyptian-exhibition-in-the-city-of-tampa/\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">Tampa Museum of Art</a>\n&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;\n<a href=\"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/tampa-museum-of-art-kicks-off-expansion-project-302518911.html\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">PR Newswire</a>\n</p>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:600;font-size:20px;line-height:1.3;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 8px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nMuseum leadership keeps turning over at the top\n</h2>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#0A0A0A;margin:0 0 10px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nZeitz MOCAA in Cape Town has finally named who succeeds the late Koyo Kouoh (d. May 2025): Elvira Dyangani Ose, most recently director of MACBA Barcelona, starts Oct 1. Separately, the Portland Art Museum has named Michael J. Anderson, president of the Oklahoma City Museum of Art, as its new director from Oct 12. Neither is a collector-founded institution, but the same bench that will run the field&rsquo;s biggest openings keeps resetting in real time.\n</p>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 26px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/08/13/elvira-dyangani-ose-appointed-artistic-director-of-cape-towns-zeitz-mocaa\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">The Art Newspaper, Zeitz MOCAA</a>\n&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;\n<a href=\"https://www.opb.org/article/2026/08/11/portland-art-museum-michael-j-anderson-oregon/\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">OPB, Portland Art Museum</a>\n</p>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:600;font-size:20px;line-height:1.3;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 8px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nA forgotten Bernini turned up in a Vienna storeroom\n</h2>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#0A0A0A;margin:0 0 10px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<em>Portrait of an Old Man</em> sat misattributed and in storage at the Kunsthistorisches Museum since 1989 for lack of a firm attribution; curators now date it to around 1670 on costume grounds, making it one of fewer than twenty accepted Bernini paintings anywhere. It anchors <em>Bernini: Painter and Sculptor</em> at Palais Lobkowitz, Dec 2 to Apr 4, 2027 &mdash; a small, cheering counter-note to yesterday&rsquo;s story about a federal collection that has permanently lost track of 111 works: sometimes the institution finds the thing instead.\n</p>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 30px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.theartnewspaper.com/2026/08/20/newly-discovered-bernini-painting-to-be-unveiled-at-vienna-museum\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">The Art Newspaper</a>\n&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;\n<a href=\"https://www.artnews.com/art-news/news/rare-portrait-bernini-newly-attributed-baroque-master-vienna-december-1234795503/\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">ARTnews</a>\n</p>\n\n<div style=\"height:1px;line-height:1px;font-size:1px;background-color:#E7E5DF;margin:0 0 28px 0;\">&nbsp;</div>\n\n<!-- WORTH READING -->\n<div style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#6B6B62;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;margin-bottom:16px;\">\nWorth reading\n</div>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:600;font-size:20px;line-height:1.3;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 8px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nDaniel Grant, &ldquo;Behind Every Museum Gift Is a Negotiation Both Sides Are Trying to Win&rdquo; (Observer)\n</h2>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#0A0A0A;margin:0 0 10px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nWhat collectors are actually bargaining for in a gift negotiation isn&rsquo;t the wall label, it&rsquo;s control over their own legacy &mdash; the unglamorous mechanics underneath every promised-gift case this brief logs, made explicit.\n</p>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 30px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.artspell.media/p/august-11-2026\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">via Art Spell&rsquo;s Aug 11 roundup</a>\n</p>\n\n<div style=\"height:1px;line-height:1px;font-size:1px;background-color:#E7E5DF;margin:0 0 28px 0;\">&nbsp;</div>\n\n<!-- TO SEE -->\n<div style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#6B6B62;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;margin-bottom:16px;\">\nTo see &mdash; escalating\n</div>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:600;font-size:20px;line-height:1.3;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 8px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nPhoenix Art Museum&rsquo;s Healey Collection show opens tomorrow\n</h2>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#0A0A0A;margin:0 0 10px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<em>The Way We Came: A Century of Indigenous Art</em> opens Aug 26, built around the William P. Healey Collection&rsquo;s 185-work gift &mdash; the largest gift of Native American art in the museum&rsquo;s 65-year history.\n</p>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 26px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<a href=\"https://phxart.org/phoenix-art-museum-receives-major-gift-indigenous-artworks/\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">Phoenix Art Museum</a>\n</p>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:600;font-size:20px;line-height:1.3;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 8px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nChristie&rsquo;s Hiroshige preview: 17 days out\n</h2>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#0A0A0A;margin:0 0 10px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nThe Alan Medaugh Hiroshige collection&rsquo;s public preview runs Sept 11&ndash;14 (sale Sept 15, 20 Rockefeller Plaza) &mdash; already on Alina&rsquo;s calendar for the Sept 12 preview.\n</p>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 30px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.artnews.com/art-news/news/christies-alan-medaugh-hiroshige-collection-auction-1234794665/\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">ARTnews</a>\n</p>\n\n<div style=\"height:1px;line-height:1px;font-size:1px;background-color:#E7E5DF;margin:0 0 28px 0;\">&nbsp;</div>\n\n<!-- FOR THE RECORD -->\n<div style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#6B6B62;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.12em;margin-bottom:16px;\">\nFor the record\n</div>\n\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Newsreader',Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:600;font-size:20px;line-height:1.3;color:#1A1A17;margin:0 0 8px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nEuropol: museum heists across Europe are turning more violent\n</h2>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.6;color:#0A0A0A;margin:0 0 10px 0;max-width:100%;\">\nA new Europol report finds thieves increasingly using sledgehammers, explosives, and firearms, sometimes hired via social media, with precious metals and Chinese cultural artifacts now frequent targets alongside paintings &mdash; a shift from the largely non-violent property-crime pattern the field is used to. Relevant background for anyone thinking about the insurance and security side of stewarding a collection.\n</p>\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;margin:0 0 8px 0;max-width:100%;\">\n<a href=\"https://www.europol.europa.eu/media-press/newsroom/news/museum-heists-turn-violent-new-europol-report-cultural-property-theft-tactics\" style=\"color:#1A1A17;text-decoration:underline;\">Europol</a>\n</p>\n\n<div style=\"height:1px;line-height:1px;font-size:1px;background-color:#E7E5DF;margin:34px 0 20px 0;\">&nbsp;</div>\n\n<p style=\"font-family:'Manrope','Schibsted Grotesk',system-ui,-apple-system,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:#6B6B62;margin:0;max-width:100%;\">\nNariway Signals &mdash; a daily read on private art collections, their legacy, and their futures.\n</p>\n\n</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</body>\n</html>\n"}